{"content_hash": "9b8af7e5216b830a", "version_id": 343, "article_id": 35, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Hidden Violence: Family Members Are Main Victims When Mental Illness Leads to Homicide", "title_zh": "看不见的暴力：当精神疾病引发凶杀案，家人往往是主要受害者", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-hidden-violence-family-members-main.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1559781732-eed3e087c660?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8N3x8aGlkZGVuJTIwdmlvbGVuY2UlMjBmYW1pbHl8ZW58MHwwfHx8MTc5MTU1MzM0NHww&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by mostafa meraji on Unsplash", "author": "mostafa meraji", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@mostafa_meraji?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/two-women-performing-drama-ZC4zXCMqpdk?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(93, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(131, 46%, 48%)", "label": "HEALTH", "seed": "51edab589bfb"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 236, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "A new study from Spain looks at homicides committed by people with mental disorders. It finds that family members are the most common victims, yet they often receive little support or compensation.", "lead_zh": "西班牙一项新研究调查了由精神障碍患者实施的凶杀案。研究发现，家人是最常见的受害者，但他们往往得不到什么支持或赔偿。", "preview": "In recent decades, how society and the law see people with mental disorders has changed. In the past, they were often considered dangerous. ", "preview_zh": "近几十年来，社会和法律对精神障碍患者的看法发生了变化。过去，他们常被认为很危险。然而，自20世纪末以来，情况发生了转变。现在，精神障碍患者本身常被视为受害者，即使他们犯了故意犯罪。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 333, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/35/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 100.862, "size_bytes": 608976, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 270, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@09df8a5/content/data/versions/343.json", "paragraphs": ["In recent decades, how society and the law see people with mental disorders has changed. In the past, they were often considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the situation has shifted. Now, people with mental disorders are often seen as victims themselves, even when they have committed intentional crimes.", "A research team from the Universitat Oberta de Catalunya (UOC) studied over 2,500 homicide cases tried in Spanish courts. About 10% of these cases involved perpetrators with some type of mental disorder. Most of these homicides happened at home, and 26% were parricides, meaning the killing of a parent.", "The study found that only a small number of victims received compensation or financial support. This is largely because most incidents happen within the family. When a person with a mental disorder attacks a family member, the harm is both physical and psychological. Other family members also suffer.", "Family members are most at risk because they have the most contact with the perpetrators. Society leaves this problem to families, and stress can make family relationships even harder. According to researcher Josep Maria Tamarit, these victims do not receive enough support from institutions.", "To prevent this kind of homicide, the public health care system should offer more support to families. This includes follow-up care when risk has been identified. Tamarit says the system should use risk identification tools and improve public support for victims of violent crime."], "paragraphs_zh": ["近几十年来，社会和法律对精神障碍患者的看法发生了变化。过去，他们常被认为很危险。然而，自20世纪末以来，情况发生了转变。现在，精神障碍患者本身常被视为受害者，即使他们犯了故意犯罪。", "来自加泰罗尼亚开放大学（UOC）的研究团队研究了西班牙法院审理的2500多起凶杀案。其中约10%的案件涉及患有某种精神障碍的行凶者。这些凶杀案大多发生在家中，26%是弑亲案，即杀害父母。", "研究发现，只有少数受害者获得了赔偿或经济支持。这主要是因为大多数事件发生在家庭内部。当精神障碍患者攻击家人时，伤害既是身体上的，也是心理上的。其他家庭成员也会受到影响。", "家人面临的风险最大，因为他们与行凶者接触最多。社会把这个问题留给家庭，而压力会让家庭关系更加艰难。研究员约瑟普·玛丽亚·塔马里特表示，这些受害者从机构获得的帮助不够。", "为了预防这类凶杀案，公共卫生系统应该为家庭提供更多支持。这包括在发现风险时进行后续跟踪。塔马里特说，系统应该使用风险识别工具，并改善对暴力犯罪受害者的公共支持。"], "body": "In recent decades, how society and the law see people with mental disorders has changed. In the past, they were often considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the situation has shifted. Now, people with mental disorders are often seen as victims themselves, even when they have committed intentional crimes.\n\nA research team from the Universitat Oberta de Catalunya (UOC) studied over 2,500 homicide cases tried in Spanish courts. About 10% of these cases involved perpetrators with some type of mental disorder. Most of these homicides happened at home, and 26% were parricides, meaning the killing of a parent.\n\nThe study found that only a small number of victims received compensation or financial support. This is largely because most incidents happen within the family. When a person with a mental disorder attacks a family member, the harm is both physical and psychological. Other family members also suffer.\n\nFamily members are most at risk because they have the most contact with the perpetrators. Society leaves this problem to families, and stress can make family relationships even harder. According to researcher Josep Maria Tamarit, these victims do not receive enough support from institutions.\n\nTo prevent this kind of homicide, the public health care system should offer more support to families. This includes follow-up care when risk has been identified. Tamarit says the system should use risk identification tools and improve public support for victims of violent crime.", "body_zh": "近几十年来，社会和法律对精神障碍患者的看法发生了变化。过去，他们常被认为很危险。然而，自20世纪末以来，情况发生了转变。现在，精神障碍患者本身常被视为受害者，即使他们犯了故意犯罪。\n\n来自加泰罗尼亚开放大学（UOC）的研究团队研究了西班牙法院审理的2500多起凶杀案。其中约10%的案件涉及患有某种精神障碍的行凶者。这些凶杀案大多发生在家中，26%是弑亲案，即杀害父母。\n\n研究发现，只有少数受害者获得了赔偿或经济支持。这主要是因为大多数事件发生在家庭内部。当精神障碍患者攻击家人时，伤害既是身体上的，也是心理上的。其他家庭成员也会受到影响。\n\n家人面临的风险最大，因为他们与行凶者接触最多。社会把这个问题留给家庭，而压力会让家庭关系更加艰难。研究员约瑟普·玛丽亚·塔马里特表示，这些受害者从机构获得的帮助不够。\n\n为了预防这类凶杀案，公共卫生系统应该为家庭提供更多支持。这包括在发现风险时进行后续跟踪。塔马里特说，系统应该使用风险识别工具，并改善对暴力犯罪受害者的公共支持。", "text": "In recent decades, how society and the law see people with mental disorders has changed. In the past, they were often considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the situation has shifted. Now, people with mental disorders are often seen as victims themselves, even when they have committed intentional crimes. A research team from the Universitat Oberta de Catalunya (UOC) studied over 2,500 homicide cases tried in Spanish courts. About 10% of these cases involved perpetrators with some type of mental disorder. Most of these homicides happened at home, and 26% were parricides, meaning the killing of a parent. The study found that only a small number of victims received compensation or financial support. This is largely because most incidents happen within the family. When a person with a mental disorder attacks a family member, the harm is both physical and psychological. Other family members also suffer. Family members are most at risk because they have the most contact with the perpetrators. Society leaves this problem to families, and stress can make family relationships even harder. According to researcher Josep Maria Tamarit, these victims do not receive enough support from institutions. To prevent this kind of homicide, the public health care system should offer more support to families. This includes follow-up care when risk has been identified. Tamarit says the system should use risk identification tools and improve public support for victims of violent crime.", "vocab": [{"word": "mental disorder", "pos": "noun", "zh": "精神障碍", "note": "指影响思维或情绪的精神健康问题"}, {"word": "homicide", "pos": "noun", "zh": "凶杀；他杀", "note": "法律用语，指杀害他人的行为"}, {"word": "victim", "pos": "noun", "zh": "受害者", "note": "常与 of 连用，如 victim of crime"}, {"word": "compensation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "赔偿；补偿", "note": "指因伤害或损失而获得的钱或支持"}, {"word": "perpetrator", "pos": "noun", "zh": "行凶者；犯罪者", "note": "指实施犯罪的人"}, {"word": "parricide", "pos": "noun", "zh": "弑亲（杀害父母）", "note": "法律术语，指杀害父母或其他近亲"}, {"word": "intentional", "pos": "adj", "zh": "故意的", "note": "反义词为 accidental（意外的）"}, {"word": "compensation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "赔偿；补偿", "note": "重复项，但常用，需掌握"}, {"word": "institution", "pos": "noun", "zh": "机构", "note": "如医院、政府等公共机构"}, {"word": "follow-up", "pos": "noun", "zh": "后续跟踪", "note": "常作定语，如 follow-up care（后续护理）"}], "title_original": "Hidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders", "summary_original": "In recent decades, the social and legal perception of people with mental disorders who commit crimes has changed. Initially, people with mental disorders were considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the paradigm has shifted at both social and legal levels, with people experiencing mental disorders often considered victims themselves, despite having committed intentional crimes.", "body_original": "Hidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders\nby\nOpen University of Catalonia\nedited by\nLisa Lock\n, reviewed by\nRobert Egan\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nIn recent decades, the social and legal perception of people with mental disorders who commit crimes has changed. Initially, people with mental disorders were considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the paradigm has shifted at both social and legal levels, with people experiencing mental disorders often considered victims themselves, despite having committed intentional crimes.\nIn this new context, a team of researchers from the Universitat Oberta de Catalunya (UOC) has analyzed homicides committed in Spain to characterize those perpetrated by people with mental disorders and study the factors that determine who the victims are and the compensation awarded in legal proceedings. Their research is\npublished\nin the\nJournal of Forensic and Legal Medicine\n.\n\"The noble aim of avoiding stigmatizing people with mental disorders as dangerous can contribute to overlooking the serious situation of those who are victims of this violence or face a greater risk of victimization,\" explained Josep Maria Tamarit, a professor of criminal law at the UOC and coordinator of the Empirical and Applied Victimology research group (VICRIM). He coordinated this research together with professors Laura Arantegui and Alba Marsol, who are part of the same group. All three teach in the Faculty of Law and Political Science.\nAnalysis of 2,500 homicides in a decade\nAs part of the study, the researchers from the Digital Transformation and Governance Research Center (UOC-DIGIT) reviewed more than 2,500 homicide cases tried in Spanish courts. Of these cases, 10% involved perpetrators with some type of mental disorder. In the vast majority of cases, homicides committed by people with some type of mental disorder occurred in the home, with 26% being parricides (involving the killing of a parent).\nAccording to the experts, the main objective of this open-access study was to shed light on a reality that has so far been overlooked: the lack of recognition and compensation for victims of lethal violence committed by people with mental disorders, an issue obscured by efforts to prevent the stigmatization of the perpetrators. In addition, the study found that only a small number of victims of such crimes received compensation or financial support, a disadvantage largely attributable to the intrafamilial nature of most incidents.\nThe issue is complex: When a person with a mental disorder intentionally assaults a family member, the consequences include both physical and psychological harm. Other family members are also affected.\nFamily ties as the greatest risk factor\nHowever, in many cases, because of this tendency to avoid stigmatizing people with mental disorders, perpetrators are acquitted or receive minimal sentences. Meanwhile, victims, both direct and indirect, are left without any compensation.\n\"Family members are most at risk because they are the people who have the greatest contact with perpetrators. Society leaves this problem in the hands of families. It is also important to consider the tensions that can arise within family relationships, which are intensified by the stress caused by living with people with these types of disorders,\" explained Tamarit, who noted that these victims \"do not receive sufficient support from institutions.\"\nThis legal and contextual situation can be \"considered a systemic failure\" because victims are not compensated. Moreover, in some cases, to obtain effective compensation, they must hire a lawyer and pursue criminal proceedings against a family member with a mental disorder.\n\"We don't have any data to report specific failures, but if one considers that more than 10% of homicides are committed by people who represent no more than 2% of the population, and whose mental health problems are known to the health care system in most cases, we should be able to improve the detection of cases involving risk and the monitoring of individuals and their families,\" said the UOC researcher.\nPreventive and reparative measures\nTo prevent this type of homicide, particularly within the family setting, support for families provided by the public health care system should be strengthened, including follow-up care for cases where risk has been identified.\nIn the experts' view, it is essential to rethink the current approach to victimization and to focus on the specific characteristics of this form of victimization, which frequently occurs within the perpetrator's closest family circle.\n\"The health care system should increase the monitoring of cases, apply risk identification tools and improve the public support system for victims of violent crime. While several reforms have been introduced to improve support in cases of gender-based violence, other needs have so far remained overlooked,\" concluded Tamarit.\nMore information\nJosep Maria Tamarit et al, Invisible harm after lethal violence: Victims of homicide by offenders with mental disorders,\nJournal of Forensic and Legal Medicine\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1016/j.jflm.2026.103173\nKey medical concepts\nMental Disorders\nHomicide\nClinical categories\nPsychiatry\nPsychology & Mental health\nProvided by\nOpen University of Catalonia\nWho's behind this story?\nLisa Lock\nBA art history, MA material culture. Former museum editor, paramedic, and transplant coordinator. Editing for Science X since 2021.\nFull profile →\nRobert Egan\nBachelor's in mathematical biology, Master's in creative writing. Well-traveled with unique perspectives on science and language.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nHidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-hidden-violence-family-members-main.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T01:22:31.644417", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "In", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.312, "sm": 100, "em": 312, "cs": 0, "ce": 2, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "recent", "s": 0.325, "e": 0.7, "sm": 325, "em": 700, "cs": 3, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "decades", "s": 0.713, "e": 1.275, "sm": 712, "em": 1275, "cs": 10, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": ",", "s": 1.275, "e": 1.525, "sm": 1275, "em": 1525, "cs": 17, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": 